PRIVATE REFLECTION: The LaTeX bmatrix has 19 columns.  With ‘|’ at columns 1,7,13,19 the three cells of each 3×3 block are at the even columns 2,4,6 / 8,10,12 / 14,16,18.  Mapping the first data row

| & 5 &  &  &  &  & | &  &  & 8 &  &  & | &  &  & 4 &  & 9 & |

gives r1c1=5, r1c5=8, r1c8=4, r1c9=9.  The remaining givens are

r2c4=5, r2c8=3;
r3c2=6, r3c3=7, r3c4=3, r3c9=1;
r4c1=1, r4c2=5;
r5c4=2, r5c6=8;
r6c8=1, r6c9=8;
r7c1=7, r7c6=4, r7c7=1, r7c8=5;
r8c2=3, r8c6=2;
r9c1=4, r9c2=9, r9c5=5, r9c9=3.

Row 1 therefore needs {1,2,3,6,7} in c2,c3,c4,c6,c7.

Column 2 contains 6,5,3,9 so r1c2∈{1,2}.
Box 1 needs 1,2,4,8,9 after 5,6,7 are fixed, so r1c3∈{1,2,3}.
Digit 3 can only be placed in c3 in row 1, hence r1c3=3.
Box 2 then forces r3c6=9 and leaves r1c4,r1c6∈{1,6,7}, r1c7∈{2,6,7}.

With r1c1=5, r1c5=8, r1c8=4, r1c9=9 the only consistent assignment for the first band is
r1c2=1, r1c4=7, r1c6=6, r1c7=2.
Hence

r1 = 5 1 3 7 8 6 2 4 9

which respects all row, column and 3×3 box constraints.  The 5 1 3 6 8 7 4 2 9 family is incompatible with the fixed givens r1c8=4 and r1c9=9 and with the column-2 3 at r8c2, the source of the disagreement in round 1.

